🐛 Bug

After performing NNC's vectorization on the innermost loop of a loopnest, the produced result become incorrect.
To Reproduce
Code to perform vectorization and print before/after stmts:
simplified_stmts = loopnest.simplify().stmts()
stmt = simplified_stmts[-1]
print(f"Original stmt:\n{stmt}")
print(f"Before vectorization:\n{loopnest.root_stmt()}")
stmt = loopnest.flatten([stmt])
stmt = loopnest.normalize(stmt)
stmt = loopnest.get_innermost_loops_for(Pconv.data())[-1]
loopnest.vectorize(stmt)
print(f"After vectorization:\n{loopnest.root_stmt()}") 

Original stmt:
for (int g = 0; g < 96; g++) {
  for (int w = 1; w < 13; w++) {
    for (int r = 0; r < 5; r++) {
      for (int s = 0; s < 5; s++) {
        for (int h = 1; h < 13; h++) {
          conv[0, g, h, w] = (conv[0, g, h, w]) + (image[0, g, (2 * h + r) - 2, (2 * w + s) - 2]) * (weight[g, 0, r, s]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

Before vectorization
...
  for (int g_4 = 0; g_4 < 96; g_4++) {
    for (int w_2 = 1; w_2 < 13; w_2++) {
      for (int r_4 = 0; r_4 < 5; r_4++) {
        for (int s_4 = 0; s_4 < 5; s_4++) {
          for (int h_2 = 1; h_2 < 13; h_2++) {
            conv[(((0 + 0 * (((1 * 14) * 14) * 96)) + g_4 * ((1 * 14) * 14)) + h_2 * (1 * 14)) + w_2 * 1] = (conv[(((0 + 0 * (((1 * 14) * 14) * 96)) + g_4 * ((1 * 14) * 14)) + h_2 * (1 * 14)) + w_2 * 1]) + (image[(((0 + 0 * (((1 * 28) * 28) * 96)) + g_4 * ((1 * 28) * 28)) + ((2 * h_2 + r_4) - 2) * (1 * 28)) + ((2 * w_2 + s_4) - 2) * 1]) * (weight[(((0 + g_4 * (((1 * 5) * 5) * 1)) + 0 * ((1 * 5) * 5)) + r_4 * (1 * 5)) + s_4 * 1]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }


After vectorization
...
  for (int g_4 = 0; g_4 < 96; g_4++) {
    for (int w_2 = 1; w_2 < 13; w_2++) {
      for (int r_4 = 0; r_4 < 5; r_4++) {
        for (int s_4 = 0; s_4 < 5; s_4++) {
{
            conv[Ramp((w_2 + 196 * g_4) + 14, 14, 13)] = (conv[Ramp((w_2 + 196 * g_4) + 14, 14, 13)]) + (image[Ramp((((s_4 + 2 * w_2) + 784 * g_4) + 28 * r_4) - 2, 56, 13)]) * (Broadcast(weight[(25 * g_4 + s_4) + 5 * r_4], 13));
          }        }
      }
    }
  }



Comparing to the correct results:
With rtol=0.0001 and atol=1e-05, found 1152 element(s) (out of 18816) whose difference(s) exceeded the margin of error (including 0 nan comparisons). The greatest difference was 1.9056527024507724e+26 (1.9056527024507724e+26 vs. -0.2777479588985443), which occurred at index (0, 95, 13, 9).

Expected behavior

Produce the same results as without vectorization

As in title. The issue raised in #50937 (comment)
Related code:



      pytorch/aten/src/ATen/SparseCsrTensorImpl.cpp


        Lines 64 to 70
      in
      76214bb







           auto empty_crow_indices = at::empty(size[0] + 1, crow_indices().options()); 




           auto empty_col_indices = at::empty(nnz_size, col_indices().options()); 




           auto empty_values = at::empty(nnz_size, values().options()); 









           crow_indices_ = empty_crow_indices; 




           col_indices_ = empty_col_indices; 




           values_ = empty_values; 





cc @aocsa @nikitaved @pearu @mruberry

Currently, only scatter_ supports the optional reduce argument. It feels like scatter (the "out-of-place" version) should also support it (at least, I see no reason why not), since their implementations call the inplace version (which makes the implementation painless).



      pytorch/aten/src/ATen/native/TensorAdvancedIndexing.cpp


